    "text": "The dispatch office hummed with the low, constant breath of the building\u2019s heating system. It was a sound so pervasive it had long ago ceased to be a sound at all, becoming instead a quality of the silence that filled the room. Outside, the December night was a seamless black, broken only by the distant, cold pinpricks of signal lights on the tracks.\n\nArthur had been a dispatcher for thirty-one years. The console in front of him was an extension of his own nervous system, a sprawling map of glowing green lines and red dots that represented the lifeblood of the region. At 2:17 AM, the world was quiet. The last commuter train had long since been tucked away in its yard, and the great freight haulers were still hours from their scheduled passages. This was the dead time, the time for maintenance, for paperwork, for the slow, deliberate rituals that kept a man awake.\n\nHe was clearing out a drawer of obsolete forms when he found it. Tucked beneath a stack of yellowed train orders was a timetable from 1988. The cover was a faded, brittle orange, the corners soft as felt. He hadn't seen one of these in decades. He opened it, the spine cracking with a sound like a dry twig.\n\nThe pages were dense with columns of numbers, the old names of stations that had since been renamed or abandoned. And there, on the page for the old Route 4, the line that used to run up through the quarry country, was a note.\n\nIt was written in pencil, the graphite",
